DIAGNOSTIC PROBE BATTERY, no graded answer. It loads the real river-zeta map.bin, pre-probes
Test.GetDensity at a 12-cell grid, then issues 12 GroupMove probes across open ground, sparse
fringe and dense cluster. The deliverable is the [Cohesion] lines in debug.log and the
[density-grid] lines in lua.log. No predicate is evaluated -- the terminal verdict only counts
how many probes were dispatched, which is a constant of the script.

WHY SKIP AND NOT PASS. Until 2026-09-01 the terminal verdict was `Test.Pass`, so this
scenario contributed a green to every `run-batch.sh --all` tally regardless of what the probes
showed. `Test.Skip` is the harness's own word for "ran, produced no graded answer"
(TestGlobal.cs) and run-batch.sh maps it to the SKIP bucket. The scenario is not hidden and
still runs; the verdict just stops claiming an answer it never computed.

SEE ALSO the sibling test-cohesion-* scenarios, which DO assert
(test-cohesion-extent-cap, test-cohesion-slot-leash, test-cohesion-cover-redirect). This one
is the map-realistic diagnostic they are tuned against, not a guard.

REMOVE THIS FILE in the same commit as whatever gives the scenario a real assertion. A PASS
against a live `skip` declaration grades STALE/RED on purpose: a scenario that STARTS grading
shows up loudly instead of silently changing meaning.

NOT EXPOSED TO expected-status.sh's KNOWN GAP. That gap is specific to `fail` declarations,
where a watchdog TIMEOUT-FAIL also exits 1 and therefore grades green. A timeout reaches
expected_status_grade as FAIL, and declared-`skip` + FAIL is RED.
